43 #edb/ref 17592186074613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073949 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073949 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073949 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073949 #edb/ref 1045 "Dividing Whole Numbers by Unit Fractions Using Models" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073949 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186074610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073949 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073949 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073949 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d1e56cd5-fc3b-5ec2-ae90-20ca4a2e732f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074610 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074610 #edb/ref 1061 172318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074610 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "26245a65-ebc8-5e42-9300-6d874c79a4ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074610 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074611 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074611 #edb/ref 1061 172319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074611 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3c05bf1b-ab93-581a-9e20-32a418b2cda1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074611 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073720 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074612 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074612 #edb/ref 1061 172323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074612 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "480191b5-d5a1-50b4-8e0c-c36a3b10da79" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074612 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074613 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074613 #edb/ref 1061 172324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074613 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "242be67e-027e-54a4-9866-c63cb081faa9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074613 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073721 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074614 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074614 #edb/ref 1061 172327 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074614 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7721ecc8-af47-5723-b955-d68c3dc4eecb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074614 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074615 #edb/ref 1061 172330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074615 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b662cd66-040d-56ae-be80-ddf72c03e975" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074615 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075043 #edb/ref 1064 "Remember that a **unit fraction** is a fraction containing $1$ in the numerator. \n\nFor example, the following fractions are unit fractions since they each contain ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{2}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{5}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{blue}{1}}{8}\n$$\n\nHowever, the following fractions are *not* unit fractions since they do *not* contain ${\\color{blue}{1}}$ in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}{2}}{3}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}{5}}{8}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\color{red}{7}}{12}\n$$\n\nIn this lesson, we will use models to divide whole numbers by unit fractions. \n\nLet's start with an example. Suppose we divide the pizza below into fifths. How many fifths will there be in total?\n\n![](images/4a/4a73488a784067fbe0a4281ef8de45209e284bcd8675f5476c14a8bdcb357712.png)\n\n \n\nSince each part is $\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}$ of a whole, we need to split $\\color{red}1$ pizza (whole) into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/9e/9e36c7b607bd6ada0018e574d52c060851837b62025429b6cfb94f98ed378ccd.png)\n\n \n\nSo, we get ${\\color{red}1} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 5$ parts in total." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075043 #edb/ref 1065 8557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075043 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "577d7682-3ed8-5103-8641-0c02702a9e08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075043 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075044 #edb/ref 1064 "The model below represents $2$ wholes. Let's use it to calculate the value of $2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5}.$\n\n![](images/bc/bceafee7d9ca5e92e69b5d33d9504f4290526b80059ad62ed26229d02712a278.png)\n\n \n\nTo calculate ${\\color{red}2} \\div \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}$, we need to divide each of $\\color{red}2$ wholes into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/cf/cf608ce8f5d8e2a47a4bebe21f198d193b10dd8f6e95436986bc954c89c60d19.png)\n\n \n\nWe get ${\\color{red}2} \\times {\\color{blue}5} = 10$ pieces in total. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2 \\div \\dfrac{1}{5} = 10\\,.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075044 #edb/ref 1065 8561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075044 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2de869cb-775d-5db4-b90d-7df0de218fc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075044 #edb/ref 1067 "Dividing Whole Numbers by Unit Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
